{
  "requirement": "When authoring evidence for a requirement, its complete fenced declaration MUST\nbe included in the mandatory input set defined by\n{cite}`requirements.authoring.mandatory_inputs`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** `requirements.authoring_input` is met. The implementation is complete and the tests cover it. Two small gaps in test marking and assertion placement do not change that.\n\n**Requirement.** When authoring evidence for a requirement, its complete fenced declaration must be in the mandatory input set from `requirements.authoring.mandatory_inputs`.\n\n**Implementation.** `AssessmentSources::prepare` in `src/authoring/inputs.rs` (lines 50–206) is marked `{impl}` for this requirement.\n- It looks up the subject among the non-term definitions. An unknown name fails before inference with \"no matching requirement or named example\".\n- It seeds the selector list with `InputSelector::Requirements { requirements: subject_name }` before any other contribution.\n- `generated_evidence_selector` never omits `Requirements` selectors, so the subject declaration cannot be dropped as generated evidence.\n- Each selector goes through `source_input`, which checks access to the declaration's file. A denied or unreadable declaration returns an error, so it cannot be silently omitted.\n- `selector.to_model_input(&resolved)` then produces the input. Existing tests show its text is the full fenced declaration, for example `[\":::{req} ready\", \"The project MUST be ready.\", \":::\"]`.\n- Deduplication happens in `insert_selector`, and the subject is the first mandatory input.\n- The rest of `prepare` is cited under other requirements (`mandatory_inputs`, `references.authoring_inputs`, `references.anchors`, and so on). I found no unmarked behavior or extra behavior.\n\n**Tests.**\n- `mandatory_git_inputs_require_staging_declarations_sources_and_terms` is marked `{test}` for this requirement. It checks that preparation fails, naming `md/requirements.md` and `git add`, when the subject's declaration file is not staged. That is the failure case for an unreadable declaration.\n- `bundle_combines_direct_references_terms_and_current_existing_selectors` is also marked `{test}`. It exercises the whole bundle, and it asserts that an example subject's own declaration is the first input.\n- Other tests in the same module assert the subject declaration directly, though they are not marked with this requirement:\n  - `anchored_files_contribute_root_node_inputs` asserts that the exact first input is the complete fenced declaration text.\n  - `unresolvable_earlier_inputs_do_not_block_reassessment` asserts that the full declaration is the only mandatory input.\n  - `only_edges_leaving_the_subject_contribute_inputs` asserts `has_requirements(\"requirements.ready\")`.\n- Together these cover the declaration being present, complete, first and deduplicated, the missing-subject error, and the access-denial path.\n\n**Minor observations (not gaps).**\n- The two tests marked for this requirement do not themselves assert that the requirement subject's declaration is present. The unmarked tests above do.\n- Because the bundle test does not make that assertion, the success-path coverage rests on those unmarked tests.",
  "references": "5d2d6c1d5124873b31ec710480f9a986971d91d25ee9fa8e7f0ee6c5a4b91f2a",
